cused on the case of a single emitter. When there are multiple emitters, the measurements association problem has not been well addressed.\n\nTo overcome the shortcoming of the conventional two-step method, direct localisation methods have been proposed in [19-25]. These methods do not need to estimate the intermediate emitter location dependent variables, but estimate the location directly. Weiss proposed the direct position determination (DPD) concept first in 2004, and derived the DPD estimator for the cases of a single emitter and multiple emitters in [19-23]. The DPD approaches make use of the cross correlation function between sensors to construct an objective function, and then search the peaks in a defined two-dimension or three-dimension grid to get the location estimate. --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nSlepian Array Processing: Concepts and Practical Considerations (https://repository.gatech.edu/items/4e15c314-d402-40a3-adc8-9536138a8b95)\nciteturn218search3 [wordlim: 200] Published: 1.9 years ago; Crawled: 4 months ago; The thesis begins with an introduction to the concept broadband source localization. ... A key part of the technique is the use of \\emph{Slepian spaces} to model signals as a union of subspaces. ... The advantage is even greater as the dynamic range between sources becomes more substantial. ... Furthermore it is shown that Slepian beamforming convincingly outperforms existing techniques of conventional and adaptive beamforming.\nThis has the capacity to greatly complicate several key problems in array processing. In turn, this motivates the development of new processing techniques that can be applied to arrays with high bandwidth and possibly a large number of elements. In this thesis, we aim to develop new techniques of broadband source localization, beamforming, and RF channel emulation. The thesis begins with an introduction to the concept broadband source localization. We then develop an iterative algorithm for localizing broadband sources. It is shown that this algorithm is applicable to localization in time, or space and time. The latter case envelopes the array processing scenario, where we wish to estimate the spectral support of an impinging signal as well as where is it coming from. A key part of the technique is the use of \\emph{Slepian spaces} to model signals as a union of subspaces. Leveraging this model, we show that our technique performs far better than standard Fourier based methods. The advantage is even greater as the dynamic range between sources becomes more substantial. --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nSubmitted my PhD thesis at QUT last week. Four years on one problem: how do you locate a radio transmitter that doesn't want to be found? Turns out, every signal gives away more than it means to… | Fraser Williams (https://www.linkedin.com/posts/fraser-williams_submitted-my-phd-thesis-at-qut-last-week-activity-7445331332857167872-bALW)\nciteturn218search4 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 3 months ago; It's called direct position determination, and it outperforms traditional methods precisely when conditions are worst: weak signals, complex waveforms, non-cooperative targets.My thesis extends this to scenarios the current literature hasn't tackled: incorporating signal strength information, handling directional emitters, processing frequency-hopping chirp waveforms, and using tensor decomposition to make fine-resolution position search fast enough to actually deploy.\n\n# Fraser Williams’ Post\n\nFraser Williams\n\n3mo\n\nSubmitted my PhD thesis at QUT last week. Four years on one problem: how do you locate a radio transmitter that doesn't want to be found? Turns out, every signal gives away more than it means to. Arrival time, direction, phase, amplitude. Process those clues jointly across distributed sensors and you can estimate position directly, no intermediate steps needed. It's called direct position determination, and it outperforms traditional methods precisely when conditions are worst: weak signals, complex waveforms, non-cooperative targets. My thesis extends this to scenarios the current literature hasn't tackled: incorporating signal strength information, handling directional emitters, processing frequency-hopping chirp waveforms, and using tensor decomposition to make fine-resolution position search fast enough to actually deploy. I'm proud of the work and keen to put it to use. Based in Brisbane, looking for roles in signal processing, RF engineering, radar, EW, telecoms, or defence R&D. I work across Python, MATLAB, and Rust, with papers from the thesis still coming. If you know of something or just want to chat about the work, I'd love to hear from you.\n\n  * `` ``\n\nGeoff Walker 3mo\n\nCongrats on this milestone Fraser\n\nLike\n\nFlorian Gast 3mo\n\nCongratulations!\n\nLike\n\nDelfina Hanna C. 3mo\n\nInteresting work, Fraser. Congrats and good luck in your search. Any firms or research projects would be lucky to have you on board!\n\nLike 1 Reaction\n\nZi Huang 3mo\n\nCongrats Fraser!! Well done! 🎉\n\nLike 1 Reaction\n\nAkila Pemasiri Hewa Thondilege 3mo\n\nHuge congratulations Fraser !!!\n\nLike 1 Reaction\n\n\nImage\n\n160 followers\n\n  * 1 Post\n\nView Profile Connect--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nLocalized spectral analysis on the sphere | Geophysical Journal International | Oxford Academic (https://academic.oup.com/gji/article/162/3/655/2097585)\nciteturn218search5 [wordlim: 200] Published: 21.2 years ago; Crawled: today; In this study, therefore, we solve Slepian's concentration problem on the surface of a sphere and obtain two classes of windows that are optimally concentrated: one spatially, within an angular radius θ_{0}, the other spectrally, within a spherical harmonic bandwidth L.Our optimization of spatiospectral localization to a zonal polar cap is the spher